"Synthesizes a research-backed user pattern into a memorable profile of goals, behavior, needs and constraints.\n\nUse when: Keep a meaningful user segment visible during product decisions. Compare needs that require different design responses.\n\nAvoid when: The profile would be based mainly on invented demographics or decorative biography.\n\nVisual grammar: Profile sections emphasize goals, behaviors, circumstances and pain points. A concise identifier helps teams discuss the pattern. Evidence and scope notes state how the persona was synthesized.\n\nBuild: Analyze research for recurring behavior and need patterns. Select attributes that affect actual product or service choices. Write a concise profile and validate its coherence against the evidence.\n\nCheck: Attributes are grounded in research rather than stereotypes. The persona represents a useful pattern, not every participant. Design-relevant constraints are distinguished from incidental biographical details.\n\nPitfalls: Adding fictional personal detail that distracts from decision needs. Treating a persona as a statistically representative average person.\n\nExample: A community class persona describes an occasional learner balancing irregular shifts, emphasizing scheduling flexibility and confidence needs.\n\nAlternatives: Empathy map, Job map."
